`dedupe_keep_order`: walk the input once with a `std::unordered_set<int> seen` beside it. `seen.insert(y)` returns a pair; `.second` is `true` only if `y` was not already there. Push to the output exactly when it is.

```cpp
std::unordered_set<int> seen;
for (int y : v)
    if (seen.insert(y).second) out.push_back(y);
```

`std::set_intersection` requires both inputs to be sorted and writes to an output iterator. Build a `std::set<int>` from each vector (sorted and unique for free), then write into the result with `std::back_inserter`.

```cpp
std::set<int> sa(a.begin(), a.end()), sb(b.begin(), b.end());
std::vector<int> out;
std::set_intersection(sa.begin(), sa.end(), sb.begin(), sb.end(), std::back_inserter(out));
return out;
```
